Repository (Hono): <workspace>

TASK: Fix the Set-Cookie merge bug in the `Context.res` setter in `src/context.ts`, and add/update regression tests in `src/context.test.ts`.

ROOT CAUSE
In `src/context.ts` the setter currently does:

```ts
  set res(_res: Response | undefined) {
    if (this.#res && _res) {
      _res = createResponseInstance(_res.body, _res)
      for (const [k, v] of this.#res.headers.entries()) {
        if (k === 'content-type') {
          continue
        }
        if (k === 'set-cookie') {
          const cookies = this.#res.headers.getSetCookie()
          _res.headers.delete('set-cookie')
          for (const cookie of cookies) {
            _res.headers.append('set-cookie', cookie)
          }
        } else {
          _res.headers.set(k, v)
        }
      }
    }
    this.#res = _res
    this.finalized = true
  }
```

The `_res.headers.delete('set-cookie')` discards the incoming Response's own cookies, so when middleware already set a cookie on `c.res` and a handler returns a new Response with its own Set-Cookie header, only the middleware cookie survives.

REQUIRED FIX in `src/context.ts`
Merge both sides without duplicating: the new Response's existing Set-Cookie headers must be preserved, and the previous `this.#res` cookies must be appended. Also avoid the current in-loop `getSetCookie()` pattern (iterating `entries()` yields one `set-cookie` entry per cookie, so appending the full old list per entry would duplicate cookies). Suggested shape:

```ts
  set res(_res: Response | undefined) {
    if (this.#res && _res) {
      const cookies = this.#res.headers.getSetCookie()
      _res = createResponseInstance(_res.body, _res)
      for (const [k, v] of this.#res.headers.entries()) {
        if (k === 'content-type' || k === 'set-cookie') {
          continue
        }
        _res.headers.set(k, v)
      }
      for (const cookie of cookies) {
        _res.headers.append('set-cookie', cookie)
      }
    }
    this.#res = _res
    this.finalized = true
  }
```

Keep all other behavior (content-type skip, other header overwrite semantics, body preservation, `finalized = true`) unchanged. Follow the existing code style (no semicolons, single quotes, 2 spaces, no needless comments).

TESTS in `src/context.test.ts`
1. The existing test `'Should set cookie headers when re-assigning Response to \`c.res\`'` (around lines 430-445) encodes the buggy behavior: it sets 2 cookies on the first response, then reassigns a response with 1 new cookie and asserts only the 2 old cookies remain. Update it to assert that BOTH survive: total length = 3 and the list contains the 2 old cookies plus the new cookie (order-insensitive assertions are safer).
2. Add a focused regression test in the `describe('Context header', ...)` block, e.g. `'Should keep both existing and new Set-Cookie headers when re-assigning c.res'`: assign `c.res` a Response carrying a middleware cookie, then assign a new Response carrying its own cookie, then assert `c.res.headers.getSetCookie()` has exactly 2 values containing both cookies. Use plain `Response`/`Headers`; do not import Hono.
3. Do not weaken or remove any other existing cookie tests.

CONSTRAINTS
- Only modify `src/context.ts` and `src/context.test.ts`. No unrelated refactors, no formatting churn.
- Never run `git add`, `git stage`, or `git commit`; leave changes unstaged.
- Do not edit files via shell redirection; use your file-editing tools.

VERIFY
Run exactly: `pnpm exec vp test --run src/context.test.ts`
It must pass all tests. Also run `pnpm exec tsc --noEmit` only if quick; if the repo has a different typecheck script that is slow, skip it and say so.

REPORT BACK
- Exact diff (or changed hunks with line numbers) for both files.
- Test command output summary: pass/fail counts.
- Any concerns.

